Helix greeter
=============

The Helix login screen, run by greetd. Installing this package does not
change which login manager is active.

Switch to it (from a text console or any terminal):

    sudo systemctl disable gdm.service      # or lightdm, sddm, ...
    sudo systemctl enable --force greetd.service
    sudo reboot

Use the real unit name: on Debian and Ubuntu "gdm3" is only an alias of
gdm.service, and "systemctl disable gdm3" fails. --force replaces the
display-manager.service link that still points at the old login manager.

Switch back:

    sudo systemctl disable greetd.service
    sudo systemctl enable --force gdm.service
    sudo reboot

If the greeter does not come up, press Ctrl+Alt+F3 for a text login and
switch back as above. Its log is in `journalctl -u greetd`
(`journalctl -u greetd -b -1` for the previous boot).

Settings: in your Helix session, Settings > Login Screen (an
administrator's password is asked; it runs
/usr/libexec/helix/helix-login-config through pkexec), or by hand: copy
/usr/share/doc/helix-greeter/greeter.toml.example to
/etc/helix/greeter.toml. Changes apply the next time the login screen
starts.

Monitors: the login screen goes on the primary monitor of the layout in
/etc/xdg/helix/monitors.toml (else the first monitor). To use your own
layout, choose Settings > Displays > "Use This Layout for the Login
Screen" in your Helix session (an administrator's password is asked),
or copy all your remembered layouts by hand:

    sudo install -D -m 644 ~/.config/helix/monitors.toml /etc/xdg/helix/monitors.toml

Layouts are kept per set of monitors, so the copy only matters for the
monitors connected at the login screen.
